/home/runner/work/cherri/cherri/cherrilang.org/language/control-flow.md:37:Precede the first condition with the optional `legacy` keyword to emit `WFConditionalLegacyComparisonBehavior` on the conditional action, matching how modern Shortcuts clients re-save older conditionals:
/home/runner/work/cherri/cherri/cherrilang.org/language/control-flow.md:168:## Automatic nothing actions at the end
/home/runner/work/cherri/cherri/cherrilang.org/language/control-flow.md:170:The `nothing()` actions are automatically added to the ending block of any statement. This ensures the control flow block does not store any output to reduce memory usage.
/home/runner/work/cherri/cherri/cherrilang.org/language/control-flow.md:181:#include 'actions/network'
/home/runner/work/cherri/cherri/cherrilang.org/language/control-flow.md:196:#include 'actions/device'
/home/runner/work/cherri/cherri/cherrilang.org/language/control-flow.md:197:#include 'actions/sharing'
/home/runner/work/cherri/cherri/cherrilang.org/language/menus.md:34:Create a variable with a `list()` action as its value.
/home/runner/work/cherri/cherri/cherrilang.org/language/menus.md:42:Then, use the `chooseFromList()` action with the list and prompt.
/home/runner/work/cherri/cherri/cherrilang.org/language/best-practices.md:23:If an action produces an output you will not use, consider adding the `nothing()` action after it. This will clear the current output, reducing runtime memory.
/home/runner/work/cherri/cherri/cherrilang.org/language/best-practices.md:37:We use the **Set Variable** action to set the variable `someArray` with no value.
/home/runner/work/cherri/cherri/cherrilang.org/language/best-practices.md:39:Then, for each value, we will create the appropriate action and input it into an **Add to Variable** action to add that value to the variable `someArray`.
/home/runner/work/cherri/cherri/cherrilang.org/language/best-practices.md:45:Shortcuts will run a Dictionary action and move on, but for an array, Shortcuts will need to run through adding each value you have pre-defined for the array to contain to the assigned variable. Depending on if you have large pre-defined values (multiple dictionaries, etc.) and how many of them you have, this may impact runtime performance.
/home/runner/work/cherri/cherri/cherrilang.org/language/best-practices.md:47:To be clear, this does not at all impact creating an empty array and then later in your Shortcut adding values to it, this is primarily to address pre-defined top of Shortcut arrays or adding to an array that already exists at the top of your Shortcut, as your Shortcut may then take slightly longer to start or perform actions while adding a lot of items to an array that you pre-defined.
/home/runner/work/cherri/cherri/cherrilang.org/language/best-practices.md:51:Notes on reducing the action count and file size of compiled Shortcuts.
/home/runner/work/cherri/cherri/cherrilang.org/language/best-practices.md:55:Using constants when applicable allows you to reference the output of an action instead of assigning it to a variable. For every variable you never mutate the value of, you can reduce your Shortcut by 1 action. This is not a lot if you have one variable, but it adds up the more variables you add to a Shortcut.
/home/runner/work/cherri/cherri/cherrilang.org/language/best-practices.md:62:Setting a value that will never change to a variable uses more actions and likely more memory at runtime to store the variable. Use the existing output of an action, do not assign it to a variable unless you will need to modify the output.
/home/runner/work/cherri/cherri/cherrilang.org/language/best-practices.md:71:If an action has an optional argument and/or default value, avoid filling it in unless you need to specify a value. If you must fill it in with its default value to set the value of an argument after it, use `nil` as the value instead. This will prevent the parameter from being set with any default value, resulting in a smaller Shortcut.
/home/runner/work/cherri/cherri/cherrilang.org/language/best-practices.md:73:Keep in mind, that setting the value of an action argument to `nil` or not setting it if it has a default value or is optional, depending on the parameter, does not mean that the value for that argument will be empty in the Shortcut, but rather that it will use the default value for that argument.
/home/runner/work/cherri/cherri/cherrilang.org/language/variables-constants-globals.md:19:Initialize a variable using the syntax below. Variables work just like they do in Shortcuts. This creates a **Set Variable** action and assigns the value to a **Text** action containing "value".
/home/runner/work/cherri/cherri/cherrilang.org/language/variables-constants-globals.md:126:Constants are the implementation of Magic Variables, as they cannot be modified. When referenced, the output of the action assigned to the constant is used (magic variable) instead of inserting a reference to a variable to which the action output has been saved.
/home/runner/work/cherri/cherri/cherrilang.org/language/variables-constants-globals.md:138:Array constants are not allowed, as creating an array value involves adding to a variable, and action outputs cannot be added to. Variable reference constants are also not allowed, as the value of a variable can change.
/home/runner/work/cherri/cherri/cherrilang.org/language/variables-constants-globals.md:178:The `Ask` global may be used for an action argument to prompt the user for input, or it can be used in an inline text variable to prompt for input or to complete text.
/home/runner/work/cherri/cherri/cherrilang.org/language/variables-constants-globals.md:180:It cannot be used as a variable value, as there is no criteria for a variable value in Shortcuts to prompt the user; it must be an action argument or an inline variable reference in a string for them to complete or edit.
/home/runner/work/cherri/cherri/cherrilang.org/language/variables-constants-globals.md:187:Keep in mind that Shortcuts may be picky about the usage of this global for an action argument.
/home/runner/work/cherri/cherri/cherrilang.org/language/variables-constants-globals.md:191:The prompt for an `Ask` used for an action argument can be set using the following syntax:
/home/runner/work/cherri/cherri/cherrilang.org/language/raw-actions.md:2:title: Raw Actions
/home/runner/work/cherri/cherri/cherrilang.org/language/raw-actions.md:8:# Raw Actions
/home/runner/work/cherri/cherri/cherrilang.org/language/raw-actions.md:11:You can write a singular definition of an action with custom parameters not defined inside Cherri, in Cherri.
/home/runner/work/cherri/cherri/cherrilang.org/language/raw-actions.md:19:## Defining an action
/home/runner/work/cherri/cherri/cherrilang.org/language/raw-actions.md:21:Actions are primarily composed of an identifier and, optionally, parameters. To add an action to your Shortcut raw, provide a string of the action `WFWorkflowActionIdentifier`. Then, optionally provide a dictionary for each of the required `WFWorkflowActionParameters` of the action.
/home/runner/work/cherri/cherri/cherrilang.org/language/raw-actions.md:24:rawAction("is.workflow.actions.alert", {
/home/runner/work/cherri/cherri/cherrilang.org/language/raw-actions.md:25:     "WFAlertActionMessage": "Hello, world!",
/home/runner/work/cherri/cherri/cherrilang.org/language/raw-actions.md:26:     "WFAlertActionCancelButtonShown": false
/home/runner/work/cherri/cherri/cherrilang.org/language/raw-actions.md:30:This defines an alert action with the message `Hello World!`. This action is already defined in Cherri using the `alert()` action. Still, this example demonstrates a simple way actions not implemented in Cherri can be used.
/home/runner/work/cherri/cherri/cherrilang.org/language/raw-actions.md:32:However, there is an alternative called [action definitions]({{ '/language/action-definitions' | relative_url }}), which creates reusable action definitions in the same way as the standard actions.
/home/runner/work/cherri/cherri/cherrilang.org/language/raw-actions.md:36:To find the identifier and parameters for an action from a Shortcut not implemented in Cherri, see [parsing out action data from a raw Shortcut]({{ '/faq#how-do-i-use-non-standard-actions' | relative_url }}).
/home/runner/work/cherri/cherri/cherrilang.org/language/raw-actions.md:44:rawAction("is.workflow.actions.documentpicker.save", {
/home/runner/work/cherri/cherri/cherrilang.org/language/raw-actions.md:49:Again, just like with the `alert()` action, there is already a `saveFile()` action; this is only an example.
